.TH BASENAME 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
basename \- strip the directory and suffix from a path
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basename
.I path
.RI [ suffix ]
.br
.B basename \-a
.RB [ \-s
.IR suffix ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B basename \-s
.I suffix
.IR path ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B basename
prints the last component of
.IR path ,
followed by a newline, with an optional
.I suffix
removed.
It works on the string alone and does not access the file system.
.PP
Both slash and backslash separate components.
Trailing separators are removed, and the result is what follows the last
separator that is left, so
.B a/b//
and
.B a\eb
both give
.BR b .
An empty
.I path
gives an empty line.
A path of separators alone gives its first character, so
.B ///
gives
.BR / .
.PP
A drive, an ASCII letter and a colon at the start of
.IR path ,
is not a component, so the drive-relative
.B C:foo
gives
.BR foo .
When only separators follow the drive, the result is the drive and the
first separator:
.B C:/
gives
.BR C:/ ,
.B C:\e\e
gives
.BR C:\e ,
and a bare
.B C:
gives
.BR C: .
A colon anywhere else is an ordinary character.
.PP
A UNC path begins with two separators, a host name, a separator, and a
share name.
The host and the share together are a root, which is never split, so
.B //host/share
and
.B //host/share/
both give
.BR //host/share ,
and
.B //host/share/dir
gives
.BR dir .
Device paths are UNC paths too:
.B \e\e?\eC:\e
gives
.BR \e\e?\eC: .
The result for two separators and a host without a share, such as
.BR //usr ,
is unspecified.
.PP
If
.I suffix
is given, and the result ends with it and is longer than it, the suffix
is removed from the end, even from a root, so
.B C:/
with a
.I suffix
of
.B /
gives
.BR C: .
The comparison is exact, so it is case-sensitive.
A result equal to the suffix is left whole, and an empty suffix removes
nothing.
.PP
With neither
.B \-a
nor
.BR \-s ,
there is one
.I path
and an optional second operand, the
.IR suffix .
Any further operand is an error.
With either option, every operand is a
.IR path ,
and each result is written on its own line, in order.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR "\-as .c" ,
and the argument of
.B \-s
may be attached, as in
.BR \-s.c .
Option parsing stops at the first operand, so a later
.B \-a
or
.B \-s
is an operand.
.B \-\-
ends the options, for a
.I path
that begins with a dash.
.TP
.B \-a
Treat every operand as a
.IR path .
.TP
.BI "\-s " suffix
Remove
.I suffix
from each
.IR path .
Implies
.BR \-a .
If given more than once, the last
.I suffix
is used.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
the C runtime expands the wildcards
.B *
and
.B ?\&
in the last component of each argument when
.B basename
starts, even in quoted arguments, as needed for commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe .
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
When the variable is unset, busybox sets it to
.BR 0 ,
so that busybox programs it starts, such as from the kit's shell, do
not expand their arguments again.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line, with a usage message on standard error: no
.IR path ,
more than two operands without
.B \-a
or
.BR \-s ,
an unknown option, or
.B \-s
without an argument.
.IP
It is also the status when standard output cannot be written, as when
it is closed.
No message is printed, and any remaining operands are skipped.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe whose reader has exited.
No message is printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Strip the directory and the suffix:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ basename C:/w64devkit/bin/gcc.exe .exe
gcc
.EE
.RE
.PP
Strip a Windows path, quoted to protect the backslashes:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ basename \(aqC:\eUsers\eme\eDocuments\e\(aq
Documents
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the C sources in a directory by name, without the suffix:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ basename \-s .c src/*.c
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compile each C source in a directory into an object file of the same
name in the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ for f in src/*.c; do
>     cc \-c \-o "$(basename "$f" .c).o" "$f"
> done
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU basename:
.IP \(bu 2
Backslashes are separators, and drives and UNC shares are roots.
.IP \(bu 2
A path that begins with two separators, a host, and a share is a UNC
path, so
.B basename //usr/lib
prints
.BR //usr/lib ,
not
.BR lib .
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-z ,
and no long option other than
.BR \-\-help ,
so
.B \-\-version
is an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Options are not recognized after the first operand.
.IP \(bu 2
A write error gives no message.
.PP
.I suffix
is compared case-sensitively although Windows file names are not, so
.B basename \-s .c MAIN.C
prints
.BR MAIN.C .
.PP
The wildcard expansion described under
.B BB_GLOBBING
can turn one argument into several.
Started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
.B basename *.c
takes the second match for a
.I suffix
when two files match, and fails with a usage message when more do.
.PP
On Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
.I path
may contain any Unicode characters.
On older versions it is limited to the ANSI code page, and in a
double-byte code page, such as Shift JIS, the second byte of a character
may be taken for a backslash.
.PP
Lines end with LF, not CRLF.
When standard output is a console, the result is converted for display
from the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B basename
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR dirname (1),
.BR realpath (1),
.BR sh (1)
